[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Capturing Arguments with ArgumentCaptor in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing unit tests with Mockito, verifying that a method was called is often not enough. Sometimes you need to inspect the actual arguments passed to the method to ensure their internal state is correct. This is where ArgumentCaptor comes into play."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is ArgumentCaptor?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"ArgumentCaptor allows you to capture argument values during a mock method invocation. Once captured, you can run assertions against these arguments using your preferred assertion library, such as JUnit or AssertJ."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where an EmailService sends an Email object to an external NotificationProvider. We want to verify that the properties of the sent Email are correctly populated."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public class EmailServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private NotificationProvider notificationProvider;

    @InjectMocks
    private EmailService emailService;

    @BeforeEach
    public void setUp() {
        MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this);
    }

    @Test
    public void testSendEmail() {
        // Given
        String recipient = "test@example.com";
        String message = "Hello, World!";

        // When
        emailService.send(recipient, message);

        // Then
        // Implementation details follow
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Using ArgumentCaptor in the Test"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To capture the argument, we declare an ArgumentCaptor for the specific class we want to capture (in this case, Email) and pass it to the verify method."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Test
public void testEmailContentCapturing() {
    // Given
    String recipient = "user@domain.com";
    String content = "Welcome to the platform";

    // Declare the ArgumentCaptor
    ArgumentCaptor<Email> emailCaptor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Email.class);

    // When
    emailService.sendEmail(recipient, content);

    // Then
    verify(notificationProvider).send(emailCaptor.capture());

    // Retrieve the captured value
    Email capturedEmail = emailCaptor.getValue();

    // Assertions
    assertEquals(recipient, capturedEmail.getRecipient());
    assertEquals(content, capturedEmail.getBody());
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Capturing Multiple Arguments"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a method is called multiple times during a test execution, ArgumentCaptor can capture all of those arguments using the getAllValues() method instead of getValue()."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"@Test
public void testMultipleEmailsSent() {
    // When
    emailService.broadcast("System Maintenance");

    ArgumentCaptor<Email> emailCaptor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Email.class);

    // Verify called twice
    verify(notificationProvider, times(2)).send(emailCaptor.capture());

    List<Email> capturedEmails = emailCaptor.getAllValues();
    assertEquals(2, capturedEmails.size());
    assertEquals("User One", capturedEmails.get(0).getRecipientName());
    assertEquals("User Two", capturedEmails.get(1).getRecipientName());
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Summary"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ArgumentCaptor only when you need to assert on complex argument properties that cannot be easily verified using standard Mockito argument matchers like eq() or any()."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer using the @Captor annotation on fields to reduce boilerplate instantiation code within test methods."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always pair capture() with verify(), never use it inside stubbing (when/thenReturn) methods as it can lead to invalid stubbing errors."}]
